#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:330
#: 1e0f192333a248a88144f09ef62da7e8
msgid "Preparing Your Simulink Model For Code Generation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:332
#: 582fc85c915e4ab48c99bda3ef98a53c
msgid "Simulink model of the controller needs to be configured before the code generation. These steps are demonstrated in the following video."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:337
#: 2a4a180c8dc743b2a210c192a2c055f9
msgid "**Step #1:** In Solver tab in Configuration, change solver type to Fixed-Type. This is mandatory for code generation to work. Change Solver to discrete(no continuous state) this will reduce generated code's flash cost and run-time load. Change Fixed-step size to one over the :ref:`SCHED_LOOP_RATE<SCHED_LOOP_RATE>`. The code generation needs to be repeated each time this parameter is changed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:339
#: 5bc33970d9724b9c8a6fca05152e3f63
msgid "**Step #2:** In the Hardware Implementation tab, change Device vendor to ARM Compatible and Device type to ARM Cortex-M. This determines which data types are available and what is their size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:341
#: c53a0148b6584b3ea1c83e31e9e360b4
msgid "**Step #3:** In the Code Generation tab, change the System target file to ert.tlc Embedded Coder and language to C++. Since we don't want Simulink to compile the generated code click Generate code only. Click Package code and artifacts to get only relevant generated code files in a single folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:343
#: d1952ffc86e44e8faa0045b7960cb9da
msgid "**Step #4:** In Code Generation->Optimization tab, change Default parameter behavior to Tunable. This will create a variable inside the generated code with the same name as they are defined in Matlab or Simulink workspace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:345
#: cbca158799e640bebc71de84532a8806
msgid "**Step #5:** To force generated code to be strictly single precision, change Import and Outport block data type to single. Since mathematical operation in ArduPilot runs in single precision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:347
#: 71507fed82924ad99db36a7e94300f52
msgid "**Step #6:** In Code Mapping->Data, change Import and Outport data visibility to public, and member access method to none. This will allow passing input and output as an argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:349
#: 3e8687c5129c40d48fd8cdd92c9006b9
msgid "**Step #7:** In Code Mappings->Data, change Model parameters data visibility to public. This will allow accessing model parameters inside the custom controller backend."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:351
#: b387084db41442b59f6e7beafc665cff
msgid "**Step #8:** In Code Mappings->Functions, change step function input and output argument type. If these are changed to pointer as shown in the video, step 6 is mandatory. The choice of how to pass input and output depends on the user's preference. Inputs are passed by pointer and outputs passed by reference for simplicity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:353
#: 558fda44a29a4458961892771ad1fc84
msgid "**Step #9:** The last step is to click on generate code button to start code generation. If a problem arises during this process, Simulink will throw an error message to explain the problem and how to fix it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:355
#: 72d02c191d2f4aa59b422b03780a5c3d
msgid "There are a lot of options in Simulink code generation in terms of class, function, variable naming, code generation optimization level, parameters access and storage method, etc. It is up to the user to change these depending on their project needs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:358
#: 164cbf39f2d74888af56356dcc3653c9
msgid "Please watch the following video to understand what each generated header and cpp files contain."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:365
#: 7078730ff41a4dbc9703292ec61b917c
msgid "Integration Generated Code Into ArduPilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:367
#: 539af02b5f594cc48d1b950b3452e4eb
msgid "The generated source code needs to be copied inside the ArduPilot and called inside the newly created custom controller backend. The steps to achieve this is demonstrated in the following video."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:372
#: a30382a84b1641f28af7ec883eb12719
msgid "**Step #1:** Copy content of zip file into a new folder inside ardupilot/libraries. Call AC_Simulink as in the example. **Step #2:** Tell waf to build this folder if the custom controller is enabled. **Step #3:** Create an object of simulink code generated class. **Step #4:** Call initialize function of this object inside backend constructor. **Step #5:** Call step function of this object inside backend update function. Arrange input and output arguments by following the similar pattern as in ert_main.cpp. **Step #6:** Once these steps are completed we can compile ArduPilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:379
#: d430ed0cacc74751825eb412fdaa8931
msgid "As long as the input and output type and how they are passed to the step function remain the same, the steps given in here and in the previous section do not need to be repeated each time we do code generation. Even if we change the controller type for example from P+PID to PID+PID, we just need to click on the generate code button and copy and paste the zip file content to the AC_Simulink folder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/copter-adding-custom-controller.rst:381
#: 94e3da27146a4631ac26a6c4b5db2000
msgid "An example of this implementation is given in this PR https://github.com/ArduPilot/ardupilot/pull/21700."
msgstr ""
